ASPIRIN 500 MG 20 TABLETS
PHARMACEUTICAL LABORATORY:
BAYER HISPANIA S.L.
PRESENTATION:
TABLETS (20 units)
COMPOSITION PER UNIT:
     ACETILSALICILICO, ACIDO ............................. 500 MG
ACTION AND MECHANISM
- [ANALGESIC], [ANTIPHRETIC], [ANTI-INFLAMMATORY] and [PLASTER ANTIAGGING].
* Analgesic action: produces analgesia by acting at the central level on the hypothalamus and at the peripheral level blocking the generation of painful impulses, by blocking the synthesis of prostaglandins mediated by the inhibition of cyclooxygenase.
* Anti-inflammatory action: inhibits the synthesis of prostaglandins and other mediators of inflammation.
* Antipyretic action: reduces abnormally high temperature by acting on the thermoregulatory center of the hypothalamus and producing peripheral vasodilation. Vasodilation increases sweating and therefore heat loss.
* Antiplatelet action: It causes irreversible inhibition (acetylation) of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the synthesis of common precursors of thromboxanes (proaggregants) and prostacyclin, PGI2 (antiaggregant). The predominance of the antiaggregant action is due to the fact that prostacyclin is synthesized by vascular endothelial cells, capable of producing new cyclooxygenase molecules after the initial inactivation by acetylsalicylic acid. In contrast, platelets (which are cell moieties and therefore lacking a nucleus) are unable to produce new cyclooxygenase molecules, so thromboxane precursors are not synthesized.
PHARMACOKINETICS
Orally:
- Absorption: After oral administration of acetylsalicylic acid, absorption is rapid and complete (absorption of the microencapsulated form is delayed, but continues to be completed). Absorption is lower in the initial stages of Kawasaki disease, then returning to normal values. Cmax is achieved, usually within 1-2 hours with single doses. It may be faster with liquid dosage forms.
Lysine acetylsalicylate is a prodrug of acetylsalicylic acid, with better solubility than this. It allows to reach plasma concentrations more quickly than with conventional forms of acetylsalicylic acid.
Food: Foods slow down the absorption rate, but not the final amount absorbed.
- Distribution: Acetylsalicylic acid and salicylic acid are partially bound with serum proteins, and mainly with albumin. The degree of plasma protein binding is 80-90%. Acetylsalicylic acid and salicylic acid are distributed in synovial fluid, the central nervous system and saliva. Salicylic acid easily crosses the placenta, and at high doses, it passes into breast milk.
- Metabolism: It suffers extensive hepatic metabolism.
- Elimination: The half-life of acetylsalicylic acid is very short (15-20 minutes) as it rapidly transforms into the digestive mucosa, liver and plasma in salicylic acid by deacetylation, the half-life of which is 2-3 hours. Salicylic acid from the hydrolysis of acetylsalicylic acid or salicylates is eliminated with urine, which is metabolized as salicylic acid (80%) and partly as conjugates with glucuronides (15%) and glycine. The formation rate of glycine and glucuronic acid metabolites is saturable. The proportion excreted unchanged is pH-dependent (urine acid: 5%, alkaline urine: up to 85%).
Dialysis: Salicylates and their metabolites are rapidly cleared by hemodialysis (there are salicylate clearance data of 35-100 ml / min). Clarification by peritoneal dialysis is slower and slower.
INDICATIONS
- [FEVER]. Symptomatic treatment of febrile states of any nature.
- [PAIN]. Treatment of pain of mild to moderate intensity, such as [CEFALEA], [ODONTALGIA], [DISMENORREA], [CONTRACTURE] muscular or [LUMBALGIA].
POSOLOGY
500 mg of acetylsalicylic acid corresponds to 900 mg of lysine acetylsalicylate.
1000 mg of acetylsalicylic acid corresponds to 1800 mg of lysine acetylsalicylate.
- Adults and adolescents over 16 years of age, oral:
* Analgesic and antipyretic: 500 mg / 4-6 hours.
The use of acetylsalicylic acid is dependent on the appearance of painful or febrile symptoms, so treatment will be discontinued when they disappear.
The maximum recommended dose is 4 g / 24 hours.
- Children and adolescents under 16 years of age, oral: Acetylsalicylic acid is contraindicated in these patients due to the risk of Reye's syndrome.
RULES FOR CORRECT ADMINISTRATION:
It is recommended to administer acetylsalicylic acid after meals or with milk, especially if gastric damage is observed or in prolonged treatments. In patients treated with high doses of chronic form, it is suggested to administer most of the total daily dose at bedtime, along with some food.
Chewable tablets may be taken with or without drinks.
The effervescent tablets will be dissolved in half a glass of water.
The envelopes should be dissolved in water, milk or fruit juices.
If the pain continues or worsens after 10 days of treatment, or if the fever does occur after 3 days, it is recommended to stop treatment and see the doctor.
INTERACTIONS
- Acetazolamide. AAS has led to increases in acetazolamide levels up to 80-200%, probably due to displacement of binding to plasma proteins. There is a risk of poisoning, so it is recommended to avoid administration. In addition, acetazolamide could lead to systemic acidosis, so it could delay the elimination of salicylates. Although no cases of this interaction with other carbonic anhydrase inhibitors have been reported, it can not be ruled out.
- Urinary acidosis (ascorbic acid, ammonium chloride, methionine) or urinary alkalinizers (absorbable antacids). AAS is a weak acid whose elimination in urine depends on the urinary pH. Those drugs that lower the pH will decrease renal elimination, while those that increase the pH will lead to increased elimination.
- Tiludronic acid. Interaction has been detected in pharmacokinetic terms, since ASA may decrease the bioavailability of tiludronate by up to 50% when taken within the hour following tiludronate. It is recommended to distance the administrations of these medicines at least 2 hours.
- Valproic acid. There have been cases of increased valproate levels associated with ASA administration. The interaction could be due to the competition between both drugs by the same renal elimination mechanism. A dose adjustment may be necessary.
- NSAIDs. Joint administration of ASA together with other NSAIDs, including coxibs, may increase the risk of peptic ulcer and gastric hemorrhage. In addition, it has been shown that ASA may reduce plasma levels of other NSAIDs, especially those with arylpropionic structure such as ibuprofen.
-Aliskiren. Possible reduction of the antihypertensive effect of aliskiren (NSAIDs act on the renin-angiotensin system). In patients with impaired renal function (dehydrated or elderly) impairment of renal function (possible acute renal failure, usually reversible) may precipitate. Caution, especially in the elderly, monitoring the antihypertensive effect and renal function.
- Antacids. Antacids may delay and decrease absorption of ASA. In addition, absorbable antacids may increase the elimination of ASA.
- Antiplatelet agents. Clopidogrel and ticlopidine may potentiate the antiaggregant effects of ASA. On the other hand, dipyridamole has increased in pharmacokinetic studies an increase of Cmax and AUC of 31.5% and 37% respectively, probably due to the inhibition of metabolism, with the consequent risk of toxicity. In the case of prasugrel, concomitant administration is indicated, since the efficacy and safety of prasugrel was studied in patients receiving ASA.
- Oral anticoagulants. The AAS has led to a potentiation of the effects of anticoagulants such as acenocumarol, with the consequent risk of bleeding, especially of gastric origin. Such interaction may be due to the hypoprothrombinemic effects of ASA at high doses (greater than 3 g) or inhibition of platelet aggregation. The administration of single doses of ASA does not seem to involve a great risk. However, it is advisable to avoid the association in patients treated with ASA for long periods, using salicylates or other NSAIDs without antiplatelet effects, and if not possible, extreme precautions and control INR.
- Anti-ulcer. Pharmacokinetic studies have shown that increased gastric pH produced by H2 antihistamines or hydrogenation pump inhibitors may increase the absorption of ASA, with the potential risk of intoxication. Patients receiving high doses of ASA may require a decrease in dosage.
- Barbiturates. AAS may increase barbiturate concentrations, with the consequent risk of intoxication.
- Beta blockers. Administration of AAS at high doses, greater than 2 g, has resulted in a decrease in the antihypertensive effects of beta-blockers. Although the cause is unknown, it could probably be due to inhibition of prostaglandin synthesis, which appears to mediate the antihypertensive effects of beta-blockers. It is therefore recommended to avoid high-dose AAS treatments in patients treated with a beta-blocker.
- Ciclosporin. NSAIDs may increase cyclosporine nephrotoxicity. Periodic evaluation of renal function is recommended, especially in the elderly.
- Corticosteroids. There is an increased risk of damage to the gastric mucosa. In addition, it appears that corticosteroids may reduce plasma levels of ASA, although the mechanism is unclear. However, it is believed that it could be due to an increase in glomerular filtration and a decrease in tubular reabsorption. For its part, the AAS
ADVERSE REACTIONS
The adverse effects of acetylsalicylic acid are generally infrequent although moderately important in some cases. In most cases, the adverse effects are an extension of the pharmacological action and mainly affect the digestive system. 5-7% of patients experience some type of adverse effect. The most characteristic adverse effects are:
- Gastrointestinal: Occasionally (1-9%): [NAUSEAS], [DISPEPSIA] and [GASTRIC HYPERACITY]. Less frequently, [ANOREXIA], [VOMITOS], [EROSION OF THE INTESTINAL EPITELIUM], [ULCERA GASTRICA], [DUODENAL ULCERA], [GASTROINTESTINAL HEMORRHAGE] ([MELENA], [HEMATEMESIS]). Gastric discomfort can reach up to 10-30% of patients with doses> 3.6 g / day, and up to 90% in patients with peptic ulcer or preexisting bleeding. The incidence is lower if the drug is taken with food, milk or antiulcer drugs. Gastric hemorrhage is generally painless, resulting in anemia due to loss of blood in the stool. Its severity is related to the dose of the drug and the stomach pH.
- Dermatologic / hypersensitivity: [URTICARIA], [EXANEMATIC ERUPTIONS], [ANGIOEDEMA], [RINITIS]; [BRONCHIAL SPASM] paroxysmal and [DISNEA] severe. Hypersensitivity reactions are more frequent in middle-aged, female patients, diagnosed with asthma, rhinitis, or a history of nasal polyps or allergies. [EXCESS OF SWEATING] (with high doses). In patients with a history of hypersensitivity to acetylsalicylic acid and other nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory agents, [ANAFILAXIA] may occur. This may also occur in patients who have not previously shown hypersensitivity to these drugs.
- Otorhinolaryngological: Depending on the dose, [OTOTOXICITY] has been observed; [TINNITUS] and mild [DEAF] usually return within 1-3 days of discontinuation of treatment. Tinnitus is common with high plasma concentrations (> 200 mcg / ml), although there are important interindividual differences. Even in patients with previous hearing impairment, it may pass unnoticed at doses> 400 mcg / ml.
- Hepatic: Rarely (<1%): [HEPATOTOXICITY] reversible (particularly in patients with juvenile arthritis, systemic lupus erythematosus, rheumatic fever or previous hepatic impairment). It manifests with elevation of transaminases and alkaline phosphatase. It is a dose dependent, not serious, although cases of very severe encephalopathy have been observed. Very rarely, [REYE SYNDROME] (in children under 16 years) of very serious consequences.
- Renal: They are very rare with therapeutic doses. Exceptionally high doses may lead to a significant reduction of renal clearance and acute [RENAL TUBULAR NECROSIS] with renal failure. There may be a transient decrease in renal function in patients with prior renal impairment. Analgesic nephropathy has been observed in patients treated with acetylsalicylic acid associated with other analgesics. It remains to be confirmed whether acetylsalicylic acid produces only nephropathy by analgesics.
- Central nervous system: With high doses, [CEFALEA], [CONFUSION].
- Hemoglobin: [HYPOPROTHOMBINEMIA] (in high doses), [LEUKOPENIA], [TROMBOPENIA], prolongation of bleeding time, [ANEMIA] and [HEMOLYTIC ANEMIA] in patients with glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency.
- Metabolic: Exceptional cases of [HYPOGLUCEMIA] in adults. The risk is greater with high doses or in severe renal insufficiency.
Treatment should be discontinued immediately in case the patient experiences an episode of deafness, tinnitus or dizziness.
Adverse reactions are described according to each frequency range, being considered very frequent (> 10%), frequent (1-10%), infrequent (0.1-1%), rare (0.01-0.1%), , Very rare (<0.01%) or of unknown frequency (can not be estimated from the available data).
- Hematologic: Common: [HEMORRHAGIA] (increased risk), perioperative haemorrhage, [HEMATOMA],, [GINGIVAL HEMORRHAGE], [GENITOURINARY HEMORRHAGE], [HYPOPROTHOMBINEMIA] (high dose). Uncommon: [ANEMIA]. Rare: Chronic post-hemorrhagic anemia due to bleeding or occult bleeding, which will present typical symptoms such as [ASTENIA], [PALIDEZ], hypoperfusion. Very rare: [BRAIN HEMORRHAGE], especially in patients with uncontrolled hypertension and taking concomitant anticoagulant agents.
- Respiratory: Common: [BRONCHIAL SPASM] paroxysmal, [DISNEA] severe, [RINITIS], [ASMA], [NASAL CONGESTION]. Very rare: [ANAFILAXIA].
- Digestive: Frequent: [ULCERA GASTRICA], [ULCERA DUODENAL], [MELENA], [HEMATEMESIS], [ABDOMINAL PAIN], [DISPEPSIA], [NAUSEAS], [VOMITOS]. Rare: [INFLAMMATION] gastrointestinal. Very rare: [GASTRIC DRILL]
- Dermatological: Frequent: [URTICARIA], [EXEHEEMATIC ERUPTIONS], [ANGIOEDEMA], [PRURITO]. Unknown frequency: [EXCESS OF SWEATING].
- Hepatic: Uncommon: [HEPATITIS] (particularly in patients with juvenile arthritis). Very rare: [HEPATIC INSUFFICIENCY] transient with [INCREASE OF TRANSAMINASE VALUES].
- Neurological / psychological: Frequency unknown: [CEFALEA], [MAREO], [CONFUSION].
- Optics: Frequency unknown: [TINNITUS], [DEAF].
- Genitourinary: Unknown frequency: [ACUTE RENAL INSUFFICIENCY], [ACUTE TUBULOINTERSTICIAL NEFRITIS].
- General: Uncommon: [REYE SYNDROME] (in children under 16 years of age with fever, influenza or chickenpox).
In patients with a history of hypersensitivity to acetylsalicylic acid and other nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory agents, anaphylactic or anaphylactoid reactions may occur. This may also occur in patients who have not previously shown hypersensitivity to these drugs.
PATIENT TIPS
PATIENT TIPS:
- It is recommended to administer acetylsalicylic acid after meals.
- It is advisable to avoid the consumption of alcoholic beverages during the treatment.
- Symptoms such as burning, abdominal pain, general unjustified weakness, cold sweating, dizziness, hypotension, bloody vomiting or blackish stools should be reported to the physician.
- If the patient is going to undergo dental extraction or other surgery, treatment should be discontinued 5-7 days before the procedure.
- It is advisable to avoid the use of chewable tablets in the 7 days after dental extractions, tonsillectomies or surgery of the buccal cavity.
- If pain and / or fever continue after 10 and 3 days respectively of treatment, it is advised to go to the doctor.
- The use of acetylsalicylic acid should be avoided for the treatment of fever in children and adolescents under 16 years of age.
SPECIAL CONSIDERATIONS:
- It is recommended to control glycemia in diabetic patients receiving high doses of acetylsalicylic acid.
- It is recommended to control renal function and hematocrit in patients receiving high doses of salicylates for prolonged periods of time.
- In patients treated with oral anticoagulants or with heparin, it is advisable to monitor coagulation.
- It is advisable to control blood pressure in hypertensive patients, because AAS may interfere with the effects of many antihypertensive drugs.
- It is recommended to administer acetylsalicylic acid after meals.
- It is advisable to avoid the consumption of alcoholic beverages during the treatment.
- Symptoms such as burning, abdominal pain, general unjustified weakness, cold sweating, dizziness, hypotension, bloody vomiting or blackish stools should be reported to the physician.
- If the patient is to undergo dental extraction or other surgery, treatment should be discontinued 5-7 days before the procedure.
- It is advisable to avoid the use of chewable tablets in the 7 days after dental extractions, tonsillectomies or surgery of the buccal cavity.
- If pain and / or fever continue after 10 and 3 days respectively of treatment, it is advised to go to the doctor.
- Do not use in children and adolescents under 16 years of age.
OVERDOSE
 At doses of more than 100 mg / kg / day for more than two days can produce salicylism. Chronic toxicity and acute toxicity can be differentiated. The signs of salicism appear when the plasma concentrations of salicylate exceed 300 mg / l.
- Symptoms: Symptoms of overdose include dizziness, dizziness, ringing in the ears, nausea, vomiting, deafness, sweating, headaches and confusion, vasodilation and hyperventilation, blurred vision, and occasionally diarrhea. Vasodilation and sweating are the result of an accelerated metabolism. Symptoms of chronic toxicity can be controlled by reducing the dose.
In acute toxicity is the alteration in acid-base balance which may influence the toxicity of salicylates, changing their distribution between plasma and tissues. The most common presentation for children is metabolic acidosis. Stimulation of respiration produces hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis. Impaired oxidative phosphorylation causes metabolic acidosis. In children up to four years of age, the metabolic component tends to predominate, whereas respiratory alkalosis is more common in older children and adults. Absorption of ASA may be diminished due to a slowing of gastric emptying, formation of stones in the stomach, or as a result of the intake of enteric coated preparations.
- Treatment: There is no antidote against salicylate poisoning. In the case of an overdose, the patient should be kept under observation for at least 24 hours, since the symptoms and levels of salicylate in the blood may not be evident for several hours. Gastric lavage overdose, forced alkaline diuresis, repeated administration of activated charcoal and supportive therapy with fluid and electrolyte administration are treated. Restoration of the acid-base balance together with hemodialysis may be needed in acute cases.
USE IN SPECIAL CONDITIONS
PREGNANCY
FDA D Category. Studies on animals with salicylates have reported teratogenic and embryocidal effects. Salicylates rapidly cross the placenta. Epidemiological studies suggest an increased risk of miscarriages and congenital malformations (including cardiac malformations and gastroschisis). During the first and second trimesters of pregnancy, acetylsalicylic acid should not be given unless it is strictly necessary, with the lowest dose possible and the duration of treatment as short as possible. During the third trimester of pregnancy, the use of inhibitors of prostaglandin synthesis may expose the fetus to cardiopulmonary toxicity (with premature closure of the ductus arteriosus and pulmonary hypertension) and renal failure, which may lead to renal failure and oligohydroamniosis. It may also expose the mother and child at the end of pregnancy to a possible prolongation of bleeding time, an antiaggregant effect that can occur even at very low doses and an inhibition of uterine contractions leading to delay or prolongation of labor . Chronic treatment with high doses of salicylates during the final phase of pregnancy may prolong and complicate labor and increase the risk of maternal or fetal hemorrhage. Therefore, salicylates should only be taken during pregnancy after a strict benefit-risk assessment and are contraindicated during the third trimester of pregnancy.
Animal studies have shown reproductive toxicity.
Acetylsalicylic acid, as well as other salicylates, are excreted in breast milk in low amounts. There is a potential risk of effects on the platelet function of the newborn, although they have not been recorded with the use of ASA. It is generally recommended to discontinue breastfeeding in nursing mothers with long-term therapy and / or high doses; However, it has been observed that occasional single doses do not appear to have significant risk for the infant.
The use of salicylates, particularly AAS, in children under 16 years of age with acute febrile illnesses, especially influenza and chickenpox, is associated with the development of Reye syndrome (encephalopathy of hepatic origin, with a high mortality rate). This syndrome is very rare, only appears in children and adolescents. The risk disappears once the ASA is stopped. Therefore, as a precautionary measure should not be used in this circumstance. Children with fever or dehydration may also be more susceptible to other forms of salicylate toxicity. Special monitoring of serum levels of salicylate in children with Kawasaki disease is recommended because the therapeutic concentrations of plasma salicylates may be difficult to achieve by altering the absorption of ASA during the initial febrile stages of the disease; As it passes the feverish state improves the absorption so if the dose is not readjusted salicylate toxicity can occur.
Geriatric patients may be more sensitive to the toxic effects of salicylates, possibly due to reduced renal function. Minor doses may be required, especially for long-term use.
CONTRAINDICATIONS
- Hypersensitivity to any component of the medication, such as [SALICILATE ALLERGY]. Cross-reactions of hypersensitivity have been reported with other NSAIDs or with tartrazine, so it is also not advisable for [NSAID ALLERGY] or azo dyes. These hypersensitivity reactions are especially common in patients with [ASMA], [NASAL POLYPS] or [IDIOPATHIC CHRONIC URTICARIA], so that in view of their potentially fatal severity, it is recommended to avoid the use of acetylsalicylic acid in these patients.
- active, chronic or recurrent [ULCERA], or in any other process that increases the risk of [GASTROINTESTINAL HEMORRHAGE], as well as in patients with a history of bleeding or [GASTRIC DRILL] associated with treatment with acetylsalicylic acid. Acetylsalicylic acid has an ulcerogenic effect, which increases the risk of upper gastrointestinal bleeding and gastric perforation.
- [COAGULATION ALTERATIONS], especially [HEMOPHILIA] or [HYPOPROTHOMBINEMIA], as well as [DEFICIT OF VITAMIN K]. Acetylsalicylic acid exerts an antiplatelet effect, which increases the risk of bleeding in these patients.
- Severe renal insufficiency (CLcr <30 ml / minute) or severe hepatic (Child-Pugh C).
- Children under 16 years of age with fever, influenza or chickenpox, since in these cases the intake of acetylsalicylic acid has been associated with the appearance of Reye's syndrome.
- Third trimester of pregnancy.
PRECAUTIONS
- [RENAL INSUFFICIENCY]. Always use the lowest effective dose. The AAS is eliminated by urine, so that in case of renal insufficiency could accumulate, with risk of intoxication. In addition, AAS has occasionally resulted in transient decrease in renal function, and pictures such as interstitial nephritis and nephrotic syndrome. A dose adjustment may be necessary. It is recommended to avoid its use in patients with severe renal insufficiency (CLcr <30 ml / min), as well as in patients with diminished levels of albumin (See Contraindications).
- [LIVER FAILURE]. Always use the lowest effective dose, due to accumulation of ASA that is metabolized in the liver. In addition, in patients with hepatic insufficiency, hemorrhages are more frequent due to a decrease in the production of coagulation factors. It is recommended to avoid the use of acetylsalicylic acid in patients with severe hepatic insufficiency (Child-Pugh class C) (See Contraindications).
- [DIABETES]. AAS could lead to hypoglycemia when given at high doses. It is recommended that blood sugar levels be monitored before and after high dose treatment with ASA.
- [DEFUCIENCY OF GLUCOSE-6-PHOSPHATE DEHYDROGENASE]. The administration of high doses of ASA, greater than 1 g per day, has rarely been associated with the onset of hemolytic anemia, and extreme caution is therefore recommended.
- [ARTERIAL HYPERTENSION] or [HEART FAILURE] not controlled. AAS could lead to fluid retention, which could worsen these diseases, especially in cases where there is no previous treatment or in which the disease has not been able to control the disease.
- [DROP]. The AAS could compete with the uratos in its elimination, being able to increase its levels. Caution is advised in patients with gout.
- [SURGERY]. It is recommended to discontinue AAS administration at least 5-7 days prior to a surgical procedure, including dental extractions, due to the risk of bleeding during the operation. Chewable tablets should not be used within 7 days following dental extractions, tonsillectomy or surgery in the oral cavity.
- Gastric damage. Acetylsalicylic acid may lead to the appearance of erosion of the gastric mucosa, which could progress to peptic ulcer, and in more severe situations to gastric hemorrhage and / or gastric perforation. This adverse reaction can occur at any time of treatment, even in patients with no history of peptic ulcer, so be aware of symptoms such as burning, abdominal pain, general unjustified weakness, cold sweat, dizziness, hypotension , Mane or hematemesis. Precautions should be taken in patients with a history of peptic ulcer. In case of symptoms of peptic ulcer or gastric hemorrhage, it is advisable to stop the treatment (See Contraindications).
Concomitant treatment should be avoided with medications that increase the risk of bleeding, especially upper gastrointestinal bleeding, such as corticosteroids, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, antidepressants of the selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor type, antiplatelet agents, anticoagulants. Treatment should be discontinued in case of melena, hematemesis, hypotension, cold sweating, abdominal pain and dizziness
And seek medical advice immediately.
- Alcohol. Alcohol should not be ingested as it increases the gastrointestinal adverse effects of acetylsalicylic acid and is a triggering factor in chronic irritation. In cases of chronic alcoholism can cause gastric hemorrhage.
- [BRONCHIAL SPASM]. In pre-existing risk factors such as asthma, hay fever, nasal polyps or chronic respiratory failure, bronchospasm may occur and induce asthmatic attacks or other hypersensitivity reactions. It can also occur in patients with other allergic manifestations, such as skin reactions, itching or hives.
DATE OF APPROVAL / REVISION OF THE SHEET
November 2013.
CLINICAL ANALYSIS
Acetylsalicylic acid may alter the values ​​of the following analytical determinations:
- Blood: Biological increase of: Transaminases (ALT and AST), Alkaline phosphatase, Ammonia, Bilirubin, Cholesterol, Creatininfosfokinase (CPK), Creatinine, Digoxin, Free thyroxine (TQ), Thromboxane binding globulin (TBG), Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH), Triglycerides, Uric acid, Valproic acid.
Biological reduction of: Free T4, Thyroid Stimulating Hormone (TSH), Thyrotropin Releasing Hormone (TRH), Free T3, Glucose, Phenytoin, Triglycerides, Uric Acid, Creatinine clearance.
Analytical Interference of: Increase: glucose, paracetamol and total proteins.
Reduction: transaminases (ALT), albumin, alkaline phosphatase, cholesterol, CPK, LDH and total proteins.
- Urine: Biological reduction of estriol.
Analytical Interference: Reduction: 5-hydroxy indoleacetic acid, 4-hydroxy-3-methoxy-mandelic acid, total estrogens and glucose.
POSOLOGY IN RENAL INSUFFICIENCY
* ClCr <10 ml / min: Avoid use.
* Hemodialysis: Dializable (50-100%).
DOSAGE IN HEPATIC INSUFFICIENCY
* Avoid in severe hepatic impairment.
